          "citation": "891 F.2d 1087 (3d Cir. 1989)",
          "court": "United States Court of Appeals for the Third Circuit",
          "year": 1989,
          "date_decided": "1989-12-15",
          "holding": "The Third Circuit held that a state child-protective-services immunity statute cannot shield defendants from federal claims, and that a warrantless entry into a home and strip search of a child by a caseworker and police required either freely given consent or exigent circumstances, precluding summary judgment.",
          "relevance": "Directly addresses a caseworker accompanied by police entering and searching without a warrant.",
          "quote": "We conclude that: 1) Pennsylvania's Child Protective Services Law cannot immunize defendants from a suit alleging a violation of a federal law",
